{"data":[{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.00544","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.00544","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.00544","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hanqi","familyName":"Yan","name":"Yan, Hanqi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hainiu","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Hainiu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Siya","familyName":"Qi","name":"Qi, Siya","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shu","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Shu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yulan","familyName":"He","name":"He, Yulan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"When Thinking Backfires: Mechanistic Insights Into Reasoning-Induced Misalignment"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-08-30T16:04:54Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-03T00:29:52Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-28T22:36:22Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09-30T01:22:41Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-13T10:53:43Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10-14T01:48:40Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-03-09T21:29:30Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-03-11T00:11:18Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"4","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"With the growing accessibility and wide adoption of large language models, concerns about their safety and alignment with human values have become paramount. In this paper, we identify a concerning phenomenon: Reasoning-Induced Misalignment (RIM), in which misalignment emerges when reasoning capabilities strengthened-particularly when specific types of reasoning patterns are introduced during inference or training. Beyond reporting this vulnerability, we provide the first mechanistic account of its origins. Through representation analysis, we discover that specific attention heads facilitate refusal by reducing their attention to CoT tokens, a mechanism that modulates the model's rationalization process during inference. During training, we find significantly higher activation entanglement between reasoning and safety in safety-critical neurons than in control neurons, particularly after fine-tuning with those identified reasoning patterns. This entanglement strongly correlates with catastrophic forgetting, providing a neuron-level explanation for RIM."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"ICLR 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.00544","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":3,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-03T03:35:16Z","registered":"2025-09-03T03:35:17Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-11T02:32:50Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.00404","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.00404","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.00404","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hengjie","familyName":"Cao","name":"Cao, Hengjie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mengyi","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Mengyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yifeng","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Yifeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ruijun","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Ruijun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fang","familyName":"Dong","name":"Dong, Fang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jixian","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Jixian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Anrui","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Anrui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mingzhi","familyName":"Dong","name":"Dong, Mingzhi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yujiang","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Yujiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jinlong","familyName":"Hou","name":"Hou, Jinlong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuan","familyName":"Cheng","name":"Cheng, Yuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fan","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Fan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fan","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Fan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tun","familyName":"Lu","name":"Lu, Tun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ning","familyName":"Gu","name":"Gu, Ning","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Li","familyName":"Shang","name":"Shang, Li","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Metis: Training LLMs with FP4 Quantization"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-08-30T08:09:08Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-03T00:19:22Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-10T09:04:12Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09-11T00:24:48Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09-29T08:05:18Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-09-30T01:42:30Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-09-30T10:14:03Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2025-10-01T00:51:28Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"4","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"This work identifies anisotropy in the singular value spectra of parameters, activations, and gradients as the fundamental barrier to low-bit training of large language models (LLMs). These spectra are dominated by a small fraction of large singular values, inducing wide numerical ranges that cause quantization bias and severe spectral distortion, ultimately degrading training performance. This work presents Metis, a spectral-domain quantization framework that partitions anisotropic spectra into narrower sub-distributions for independent quantization, thereby reducing errors and preserving spectral structure. To minimize overhead, Metis leverages two key properties of the dominant spectral subspace: preservation via sparsely random sampling and preservation via random projection, reducing decomposition cost to a negligible level. On LLaMA-3 8B trained with 100B tokens, Metis enables robust W4A4G4 training with FP4 quantization of weights, activations, and gradients, yielding only a 0.4% training loss gap and a 0.1% degradation in downstream accuracy relative to BF16. Beyond matching BF16 fidelity, Metis also surpasses our implementation of Nvidia's recently announced (yet to be publicly released) FP4 recipe, consistently achieving lower loss and higher downstream accuracy while incurring significantly lower computational overhead. The code implementation for Metis is available at: https://anonymous.4open.science/r/Metis-quantization-644B."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.00404","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":3,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-03T03:31:58Z","registered":"2025-09-03T03:31:58Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-01T03:03:03Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.00388","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.00388","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.00388","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xuelin","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Xuelin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiangqi","familyName":"Jin","name":"Jin, Xiangqi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Linfeng","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Linfeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"GraphKV: Breaking the Static Selection Paradigm with Graph-Based KV Cache Eviction"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-08-30T06:56:28Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-03T00:18:06Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Efficient Key-Value (KV) cache management is essential for processing long text sequences in large language models (LLMs), where memory constraints often limit performance. Conventional KV eviction strategies, such as top-k selection based on attention scores, depend on static heuristics that fail to capture the evolving implicit dependencies among tokens during inference. To overcome this, we propose GraphKV, a graph-based framework that redefines token selection for KV cache compression. In GraphKV, tokens are modeled as nodes with importance scores, and edges represent their similarity relationships. Through a decay-signal-propagation mechanism, token importance is dynamically updated by propagating information across the graph, enabling adaptive retention of the most contextually significant tokens. GraphKV can be seamlessly utilized in existing KV cache eviction methods such as SnapKV and PyramidKV in a plug-and-play manner. Codes will be released on Github."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.00388","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-03T03:31:34Z","registered":"2025-09-03T03:31:34Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-03T03:31:34Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}}],"meta":{"total":3,"totalPages":1,"page":1},"links":{"self":"https://api.datacite.org/dois?query=id%3a%28%2210.48550%2farxiv.2509.00388%22+OR+%2210.48550%2farxiv.2509.00404%22+OR+%2210.48550%2farxiv.2509.00544%22%29\u0026page[size]=100"}}